('Why a weekly spa night is worth the hour', "<p>Most of us rush through skincare in two minutes, standing at the sink and half-reading our phones. A spa night flips that: the same few products, used slowly, with your full attention. The slow cleanse removes more of the day's sunscreen and makeup than a quick wash. Massaging in a rich cream feels good on tired facial muscles. And the simple act of setting aside an evening is, for many people, the most restorative part.</p><p>It also makes your products go further. When you take time with each step, you tend to use the right amount instead of too much, and you notice how your skin responds, which helps you decide what is actually worth repurchasing.</p>"),
('A sample spa night timeline', '<ol><li><strong>0:00</strong> Dim the lights, light a candle or start a diffuser, run a warm cloth under the tap.</li><li><strong>0:05</strong> Slow cleanse with the balm, finishing with the warm cloth.</li><li><strong>0:10</strong> LED mask session, lying back with your eyes closed and music on.</li><li><strong>0:25</strong> Facial massage with the night cream: forehead, cheeks, jaw, then neck.</li><li><strong>0:30</strong> Lip mask, a glass of water or herbal tea, and a book instead of a screen.</li><li><strong>0:45</strong> Into bed on the silk pillowcase.</li></ol><p>No device? Skip step three and spend the time on a longer massage or a warm bath instead. The ritual still works.</p>'),
('Choosing beauty tech wisely', '<p>Beauty devices are the biggest purchase in a routine like this, so it is worth being careful. Read the full product listing and manual before buying, including who should not use the device. Look at the number of ratings as well as the average: a newer device with around a thousand ratings is promising but has a shorter track record than a skincare staple with tens of thousands. Finally, plan to use it consistently. Most at-home devices are designed for regular sessions over weeks, and they will not do much sitting in a drawer.</p>'),
